<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Patch>
  <!--
    Keeps Vanilla Factions Expanded - Medieval 2's two default realms out of a new
    world: the civil kingdom (VFEM2_KingdomCivil) and the rough clan
    (VFEM2_ClanRough). Both declare startingCountAtWorldCreation 1, so the world
    creation page pre-adds one instance of each and every new world starts with a
    kingdom and a clan already founded and expanding.

    Both fields are set to 0. The configurable limits (maxConfigurableAtWorldCreation
    9999) are left alone, so both stay selectable by hand at world creation - they just
    no longer start there by themselves. This is the same treatment
    DisableVisibleFactions.xml gives the vanilla factions and
    DisableMedievalOverhaulFactions.xml gives Medieval Overhaul's.

    requiredCountAtGameStart is 0 on both defs, inherited from their abstract bases
    (VFEM2_MedievalFactionBase / VFEM2_VikingBase), so nothing force-adds them once the
    starting count is 0: FactionGenerator.InitializeFactions only adds a def whose
    required count is above 0.

    Deliberately NOT targeted, so a future edit here does not overreach:
      * VFEM2_KingdomRough, VFEM2_KingdomSavage, VFEM2_CivilClan, VFEM2_ClanSavage -
        already startingCountAtWorldCreation 0, so they never spawn on their own.
      * VFEM2_MerchantGuild - requiredCountAtGameStart 1, and VFE Medieval 2 marks it
        hidden: it is a faction with no settlements that exists to send the guild's
        caravans and sites, so holding it back would take that content away.
      * VFEM2_PlayerKingdom - a player start option, not a world faction.

    The defs are only held back, never removed: PawnKindDefs_Kingdom.xml names
    VFEM2_KingdomCivil as its defaultFactionType, and VFE Medieval 2's RoyaltyPatch
    lists both defNames on the Empire's permanentEnemyToEveryoneExcept, so anything
    that reads the defs still resolves. Nothing in VFEMedieval.dll touches faction
    generation either (checked: no FactionGenerator, no ConfigurableFactions), so the
    counts above are the whole mechanism.

    Each field gets a replace plus an add-if-absent pair: the replace catches the
    inline declaration (startingCountAtWorldCreation is inline on both defs), and the
    add writes the 0 out explicitly on a def that only inherits the field, so a future
    change to the shared base cannot bring the realm back silently.

    Deliberately no PatchOperationFindMod guard: it compares mod display names rather
    than package ids, so a guard written against a package id never matches and turns
    the whole patch into a silent no-op (see DisableMedievalOverhaulFactions.xml). With
    VFE Medieval 2 absent every xpath below simply matches nothing, and each
    operation's success=Always keeps that quiet.
  -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationSequence">
    <success>Always</success>
    <operations>

      <li Class="PatchOperationReplace">
        <success>Always</success>
        <xpath>Defs/FactionDef[defName="VFEM2_KingdomCivil" or defName="VFEM2_ClanRough"]/requiredCountAtGameStart</xpath>
        <value>
          <requiredCountAtGameStart>0</requiredCountAtGameStart>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <success>Always</success>
        <xpath>Defs/FactionDef[(defName="VFEM2_KingdomCivil" or defName="VFEM2_ClanRough") and not(requiredCountAtGameStart)]</xpath>
        <value>
          <requiredCountAtGameStart>0</requiredCountAtGameStart>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationReplace">
        <success>Always</success>
        <xpath>Defs/FactionDef[defName="VFEM2_KingdomCivil" or defName="VFEM2_ClanRough"]/startingCountAtWorldCreation</xpath>
        <value>
          <startingCountAtWorldCreation>0</startingCountAtWorldCreation>
        </value>
      </li>

      <li Class="PatchOperationAdd">
        <success>Always</success>
        <xpath>Defs/FactionDef[(defName="VFEM2_KingdomCivil" or defName="VFEM2_ClanRough") and not(startingCountAtWorldCreation)]</xpath>
        <value>
          <startingCountAtWorldCreation>0</startingCountAtWorldCreation>
        </value>
      </li>

    </operations>
  </Operation>
</Patch>
